Learning Log

9/10
Loss: one number that says how wrong the model is. Lower is better.
Gradient descent: repeatedly adjusting weights in the direction that reduces the loss. Update rule: x' = x - (lr*slope).
Slope (gradient): tells which way the loss grows, so we move the opposite way.
Learning rate (lr): the step size, chosen by me. Too big and it overshoots, too small and it is slow.

10/10
Weight: how much one particular input matters to a neuron.
Bias: a constant added to a neuron's sum, so it can have a value even if the weighted input is zero.
Activation function: a function applied to a neuron's sum before passing it on. It adds the bend that lets deep networks learn complex patterns.
ReLU: an activation that outputs the sum if it is positive, otherwise 0.
Epoch: one full pass over the training images.
Overfitting: the model memorizes the training images instead of learning general patterns. Sign: training accuracy rises while validation accuracy stalls or drops.
Training, validation and test set: training is the data the model learns from, validation is a mock test used to compare ideas, test is the hidden final exam never used for learning.

11/10
Softmax: converts raw scores into probabilities that add up to 1.
Cross entropy loss: the negative of ln(probability given to the correct class). Confident and right gives a small loss, confident and wrong gives a big loss.
Kernel (filter): a small grid of weights that slides over an image, multiplying and summing at each position to detect a pattern.
Weight sharing: the same kernel weights are reused at every position, so convolutions need very few parameters.
Output size of a convolution: (input size plus 2 times padding minus kernel size) divided by stride, plus 1.
